---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../components/DocCard.astro';

const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs', active: true },
  { title: 'Getting Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' },
  { title: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' },
  { title: 'Honeypot Engine', href: '/docs/engine' },
  { title: 'Detection', href: '/docs/detection' },
  { title: 'Observability', href: '/docs/observability' },
  { title: 'Operations', href: '/docs/operations' },
  { title: 'Testing', href: '/docs/testing' },
  { title: 'Reference', href: '/docs/reference' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'start', text: 'Start Here', level: 2 },
  { id: 'sections', text: 'Documentation Sections', level: 2 },
  { id: 'paths', text: 'Reading Paths', level: 2 },
  { id: 'scope', text: 'What This Documentation Covers', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Documentation" description="Technical documentation for PressHaven.io" section="Docs" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    This knowledge hub documents PressHaven.io: a <strong>defensive security platform</strong> for a
    WordPress/WooCommerce storefront behind an OpenResty reverse proxy that scores every request in Lua and diverts
    suspicious sessions into isolated honeypot pools. Today it is a high-interaction honeypot; the goal is to grow
    into a <strong>security management layer above WordPress + WooCommerce instances</strong>. The pages are written
    against the modules and deployment files in
    <a href="https://github.com/presshaven/presshaven" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/presshaven/presshaven</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Defensive only.</strong> Everything here operates on the defender's own infrastructure: it observes,
    diverts and records. The system performs <strong>no offensive tracking and no offensive security</strong> — no
    hack-back, no beacons or callbacks to adversary machines, no retaliation. Deception serves solely to protect the
    real store.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start">Start Here</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Getting Started"
      href="/docs/getting-started"
      icon="terminal"
      tag="New here?"
      description="Prerequisites, the dashboard setup path, the manual Docker Compose path, and the first checks that prove the stack is alive."
      meta="~10 min read"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Architecture"
      href="/docs/architecture"
      icon="layers"
      tag="Mental model"
      description="The pure/adapter split, the request pipeline, the twelve-stage router, pooling and the GDPR identity path."
      meta="Start with the pipeline"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="sections">Documentation Sections</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Honeypot Engine"
      href="/docs/engine"
      icon="box"
      tag="WordPress layer"
      description="Production and pool shops, the two deception layers, seeding, content replication, backups, dashboard-driven WordPress management and the lifecycle of an isolated pool."
      meta="6 sections"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Detection"
      href="/docs/detection"
      icon="radar"
      tag="6 pages"
      description="Pattern and header analysis, seeded CVEs, Suricata correlation, the generated pattern catalogue, AbuseIPDB mapping, escalation-aware decay and the device signature."
      meta="13 CVEs · WSTG mapped"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Observability"
      href="/docs/observability"
      icon="chart"
      tag="3 pages"
      description="Structured security events, the mTLS Vector → Elasticsearch → Kibana path, dashboard generation and the request timeline."
      meta="6 dashboards"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Operations"
      href="/docs/operations"
      icon="wrench"
      tag="5 pages"
      description="Two-host and single-host deployment, hardening, the full configuration reference and troubleshooting runbooks."
      meta="2 topologies"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="AI-Era Defense"
      href="/docs/ai-defense"
      icon="shieldAlert"
      tag="6 pages"
      description="Keeping control of a shop when frontier AI models find new open-source vulnerabilities faster than patches ship: allowlisting, attack-surface hardening, deception as a 0-day sensor, capture-to-virtual-patch and the Black October (PHP2Shell, Node2Shell) case study."
      meta="No defender-side model needed"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Testing"
      href="/docs/testing"
      icon="flask"
      tag="2 pages"
      description="Twenty-three pure-Lua unit test files, dashboard tests, scenario scripts and the blind pentest protocol."
      meta="23 test files"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Reference"
      href="/docs/reference"
      icon="book"
      tag="6 pages"
      description="Glossary, known issues with workarounds, roadmap, changelog, primary literature and module tables."
      meta="Glossary · roadmap"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="paths">Reading Paths</h2>
  <p>Three common ways through the material, depending on what you need to do.</p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard
      title="Operate the system"
      href="/docs/operations/two-host"
      icon="server"
      tag="Operator"
      description="Deployment topologies, hardening, configuration defaults and the runbooks for services, incidents and stale dashboards."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Understand detection"
      href="/docs/detection/capabilities"
      icon="target"
      tag="Analyst"
      description="How each signal is scored, which CVEs are seeded, how Suricata feeds the score and when a reason is reportable."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Change the code"
      href="/docs/architecture"
      icon="code"
      tag="Developer"
      description="Where the pure rules live, which adapter performs the I/O and how the routing decision is composed from predicates."
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="scope">What This Documentation Covers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Edge behaviour</strong> — request scoring, routing, session identity, pool assignment and response headers.</li>
    <li><strong>Deception environment</strong> — the WordPress/WooCommerce layer, seeding, content sync and backups.</li>
    <li><strong>Telemetry</strong> — security event schema, Suricata ingestion, Vector pipelines and Kibana saved objects.</li>
    <li><strong>Operations</strong> — two-host and single-host layouts, hardening, environment variables and recovery procedures.</li>
    <li><strong>Validation</strong> — unit suites, scenario scripts, dashboard tests and the blind pentest methodology.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Historical notes and corrections are tracked in the <a href="/docs/reference/changelog">changelog</a> and
    <a href="/docs/reference/known-issues">known issues</a> pages. The complete function-level reference is kept
    alongside the Lua modules; this hub links to the relevant module for every behaviour it describes.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
